<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n17332" root="زأن" body-key="z">
    
      <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَزْأَنِىٌّ</span></b>  and ↓ <b><span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَزْأَنِىٌّ</span></b> , applied as epithets to a spear,
are dial. vars. of <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يَزَنِىٌّ</span> (K, TA) and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَزَنِىٌّ</span>: (TA:)
spears being thus called in relation to <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">ذُو يَزَنٍ</span>, one
of the Kings of Himyer: (S in art. <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">يزن</span>:) as also
<span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">آزَنِىٌّ</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-ar" lang="ara">أَيْزَنِىٌّ</span>, both formed by transposition.
(TA.)

